 ## Background
-The concept of Level-of-Detail (LOD) in volume rendering originated in the early 2000s with work on unstructured grids, where hierarchical voxelization and adaptive octrees were used to maintain interactivity [S3]. The specific technique of "Dynamic Resolution Adaptation" via frame-time feedback is a common pattern in real-time graphics engines, often cited in mixed reality and VR contexts to maintain high refresh rates (90 Hz+) on head-mounted displays [S1]. More recently, with the rise of Neural Radiance Fields (NeRF) and 3D Gaussian Splatting, dynamic resolution adaptation has been adapted for training-time optimization, where voxel grid resolution and alpha masks are updated periodically to manage memory and convergence [S1][S2, unofficial]. The "Focus and Context" approach has roots in interactive visualization systems aiming to provide orientation cues while focusing computational resources on specific data probes [S1].
+The concept of Level-of-Detail (LOD) in volume rendering originated from the need to handle large unstructured grids and voxel datasets interactively. Early work by Leven et al. (2002) introduced hierarchical 3D voxelization and adaptive octree structures to manage memory and rendering load [S3]. The field has since expanded to include dynamic resolution adaptation for neural radiance fields (NeRFs) and implicit representations, where resolution is adjusted during training to balance detail and computational cost [S1]. In real-time graphics, techniques like mixed-resolution rendering and frame splitting have been adopted to handle complex volumetric effects like clouds in games [S2, unofficial]. In Virtual Reality, Multiresolution Atlas Buffers (MAB) have been developed to mitigate the high performance demands of stereoscopic volume rendering by leveraging peripheral vision and lens distortion characteristics [S1].
 
